('domain', 'D078', 'D078', 'method', 'd078-qualitative-approach', 'Qualitative methods (working with words, observations and meaning)', 'Qualitative research studies meanings, experiences and context using material such as interview answers, observations or documents. It can explain how people describe an issue, but a few interviews do not by themselves show how common a view is.', 'Choose this approach when a question asks about meaning, experience, interaction, language or context using sources such as words, observations or documents; it is not a requirement for every route.', 'A small set of coded excerpts with a clear explanation of how they address a question; practice only with permitted or fictional material.', 'build_next', 'Choose this approach when a question asks about meaning, experience, interaction, language or context using sources such as words, observations or documents; it is not a requirement for every route. Do not equate qualitative research only with interviews or imply that qualitative evidence is merely anecdotal; the design, sampling, analysis and limits still matter. Optional domain-enrichment choice for the named task only; not required for every D078 learner or occupation, not proof of competence, and not claimed to be taught by the Sociology programme.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['unesco_definition', 'openlearn_interviews']::text[], '[{"id":"unesco_definition","title":"Social science research","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/query-list/s/social-science-research","region":null,"evidenceType":"item_definition_or_task_identity"},{"id":"openlearn_interviews","title":"Conducting qualitative interviews: an introduction","url":"https://www.open.edu/openlearn/education-development/conducting-qualitative-interviews-introduction/content-section-0","region":null,"evidenceType":"independent_or_task_specific_explanation_candidate"}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"unesco_definition","title":"Social science research","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/query-list/s/social-science-research","locator":"qualitative and quantitative as varied methods","claimRole":"item_identity"},{"sourceId":"unesco_definition","title":"Social science research","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/query-list/s/social-science-research","locator":"qualitative and quantitative as varied methods","claimRole":"checkpoint_fit"},{"sourceId":"openlearn_interviews","title":"Conducting qualitative interviews: an introduction","url":"https://www.open.edu/openlearn/education-development/conducting-qualitative-interviews-introduction/content-section-0","locator":"Course introduction and sections on preparation, conducting and recording qualitative interviews.","claimRole":"plain_meaning"}]'::jsonb, 'global', '["Qualitative methods are not one single technique.","Taguette supports document coding but does not make an interpretation valid.","Domain enrichment only. The programme-to-domain relationship does not mean this item is taught by the Sociology degree."]'::jsonb, 'source_supported_synthesis', 'medium', 'source_backed', 'linked', 'https://www.open.edu/openlearn/education-development/conducting-qualitative-interviews-introduction/content-section-0', 'Open: Conducting qualitative interviews: an introduction', '{"source_id":"openlearn_interviews","title":"Open: Conducting qualitative interviews: an introduction","publisher":null,"url":"https://www.open.edu/openlearn/education-development/conducting-qualitative-interviews-introduction/content-section-0","locator":"Course content: 1.1 when interviews are useful; 2.1–2.5 preparation and ethics; 3.1–3.4 asking questions, consent and ending; 4.1–4.5 fieldnotes and data handling.","accessed_on":"2026-10-08","scope_limit":null}'::jsonb, '2026-10-08', 'medium', '{"url":"https://www.open.edu/openlearn/education-development/conducting-qualitative-interviews-introduction/content-section-0","locator":"Course content: 1.1 when interviews are useful; 2.1–2.5 preparation and ethics; 3.1–3.4 asking questions, consent