('domain', 'D078', 'D078', 'method', 'focus_groups_and_workshops', 'Focus groups and workshops', 'Use a guided group discussion to learn how people explain, agree, disagree and build on one another''s views.', 'Study how people discuss a topic together and how group interaction shapes evidence.', 'A moderator guide, ethically managed session record and analysis showing both what participants said and how group interaction shaped the evidence.', 'build_next', 'Study how people discuss a topic together and how group interaction shapes evidence. Not a universal interview substitute; a group discussion does not produce representative views by default. Optional domain-enrichment choice for the named task only; not required for every D078 learner or occupation, not proof of competence, and not claimed to be taught by the Sociology programme.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['casrai_focus_group_definition_2026', 'esrc_social_science_training_guidelines_2022', 'nuc_social_sciences_ccmas_2023_research_curriculum', 'uni_ni_qualitative_data_focus_groups_2026']::text[], '[{"id":"casrai_focus_group_definition_2026","title":"Focus Groups: Design, Moderation, Analysis","url":"https://casrai.org/guides/focus-group-research-method","region":null,"evidenceType":null},{"id":"esrc_social_science_training_guidelines_2022","title":"ESRC Postgraduate Training and Development Guidelines 2022","url":"https://www.ukri.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/09/ESRC-281123-PostgraduateTrainingDevelopmentGuidelines2022.pdf","region":null,"evidenceType":null},{"id":"nuc_social_sciences_ccmas_2023_research_curriculum","title":"Social Sciences CCMAS 2023: research-methods curriculum examples","url":"https://www.nuc.edu.ng/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Social-Sciences-CCMAS-FINAL-2023-A.pdf","region":null,"evidenceType":null},{"id":"uni_ni_qualitative_data_focus_groups_2026","title":"Qualitative Data: Focus Groups, Discourse Analysis and Conversation Analysis","url":"https://researchmethods.uni.edu/chapter-five-qualitative-methods/chapter-five-qualitative-data-part-2","region":null,"evidenceType":null}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"casrai_focus_group_definition_2026","title":"Focus Groups: Design, Moderation, Analysis","url":"https://casrai.org/guides/focus-group-research-method","locator":"Definition and sections on interaction as data, group composition, moderation and analysis","claimRole":"item_identity"},{"sourceId":"esrc_social_science_training_guidelines_2022","title":"ESRC Postgraduate Training and Development Guidelines 2022","url":"https://www.ukri.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/09/ESRC-281123-PostgraduateTrainingDevelopmentGuidelines2022.pdf","locator":"Annex I, printed p. 22 (social-science researcher learning outcomes); Annex II, printed p. 23 (research questions, qualitative/quantitative methods, sampling and case selection, generalisability, validity, reliability and replicability).","claimRole":"checkpoint_fit"},{"sourceId":"nuc_social_sciences_ccmas_2023_research_curriculum","title":"Social Sciences CCMAS 2023: research-methods curriculum examples","url":"https://www.nuc.edu.ng/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Social-Sciences-CCMAS-FINAL-2023-A.pdf","locator":"Printed pp. 177–178, Sociology Research Method II (proposal, data collection/organisation, pilot study, report and statistics); printed p. 214, Political Science research methods (research questions, instruments, data collection and analysis).","claimRole":"checkpoint_fit"},{"sourceId":"uni_ni_qualitative_data_focus_groups_2026","title":"Qualitative Data: Focus Groups, Discourse Analysis and Conversation Analysis","url":"https://researchmethods.uni.edu/chapter-five-qualitative-methods/chapter-five-qualitative-data-part-2","locator":"Sections introducing focus groups and the distinction between individual answers and group interaction","claimRole":"plain_meaning"}]'::jsonb, 'global', '["Group discussion can be shaped by dominant voices, silence, conformity, privacy limits and the group composition.","Findings from