_item_disposition","decision_id":"ai_assisted_and_digital_trace_research_ethics"}'::jsonb, 'free_task_specific_learning_resource', 'Introductory-to-intermediate ethics guide', 'English', 'Free official guidance', NULL, 'deferred_emerging_conditional_extension', 'Deferred emerging conditional extension', 'deferred_emerging_conditional_extension', 'useful', 'Assess human oversight, data quality, provenance, consent, privacy, bias and disclosure when AI or online traces are used. Keep AI as a conditional method/data-use extension, not an occupation, replacement for participants, or general research requirement. Optional domain-enrichment choice for the named task only; not required for every D078 learner or occupation, not proof of competence, and not claimed to be taught by the Sociology programme.'),
('domain', 'D078', 'D078', 'tool', 'd078_python', 'Python', 'Python is a programming language used to write repeatable instructions for handling data, text, networks, visualisations and simulations. In this domain it is a flexible implementation option, not a social-science method by itself.', 'Write repeatable code for data preparation, analysis, visualisation, text, networks or simulation.', 'A documented script/notebook plus data transformations, tables/figures or model results and a reproducibility note.', 'build_next', 'Write repeatable code for data preparation, analysis, visualisation, text, networks or simulation. Language syntax alone does not teach research design, statistics, social theory, responsible data practice or valid interpretation. Optional domain-enrichment choice for the named task only; not required for every D078 learner or occupation, not proof of competence, and not claimed to be taught by the Sociology programme.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['url_a17e5b24c217aa80', 'url_4971ef18227673ce', 'programming_historian_python_sentiment_2026']::text[], '[{"id":"url_a17e5b24c217aa80","title":"The Python Tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/","region":null,"evidenceType":null},{"id":"url_4971ef18227673ce","title":"Data Carpentries: Python for social scientists","url":"https://datacarpentry.org/python-socialsci/","region":null,"evidenceType":null},{"id":"programming_historian_python_sentiment_2026","title":"Use Python for text analysis in a research workflow","url":"https://programminghistorian.org/en/lessons/sentiment-analysis","region":"global","evidenceType":"task_specific_learning_resource"}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"url_a17e5b24c217aa80","title":"The Python Tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/","locator":"Language syntax, data structures, modules, input/output, errors, virtual environments and packages","claimRole":"item_identity"},{"sourceId":"url_a17e5b24c217aa80","title":"Python tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/","locator":"The tutorial teaches language operation but explicitly expects basic programming; domain methods remain separate","claimRole":"checkpoint_fit"},{"sourceId":"url_4971ef18227673ce","title":"Data Carpentries: Python for social scientists","url":"https://datacarpentry.org/python-socialsci/","locator":"Social-science workflow for importing, cleaning, summarising and plotting data","claimRole":"plain_meaning"}]'::jsonb, 'global', '["Learning syntax alone does not teach research design, statistics or social theory.","Domain enrichment only. The programme-to-domain relationship does not mean this item is taught by the Sociology degree."]'::jsonb, 'source_supported_synthesis', 'medium', 'source_backed', 'linked', 'https://programminghistorian.org/en/lessons/sentiment-analysis', 'Use Python for text analysis in a research workflow', '{"source_id":"programming_historian_python_sentiment_2026","title":"Use Python for text analysis in a research workflow","publisher":null,"url":"https://programminghistorian.org/en/lessons/sentiment-analysis","locator":"Lesson sections on loading text, selecting an NLP approach, calculating sentiment scores and interpreting results","accessed_on":"2026-10-09","scope_limit":null}'::jsonb, '2026-10-09', 'medium', '{"url":"https://programminghistorian.org/en/lessons/sentiment-analysis","locator":"Lesson sections on loading text, selecting an NLP approach, calculating sentiment scores and interpreting results","supports":"Task-specific learning resource for the item","access":"Free online lesson and code examples"}'::jsonb, NULL, 'domain', '2026-10-12', 'approved', '{"claims":{"item_identity":{"source_references":[{"id":"url_a17e5b24c217aa80","title":"The Python Tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/","region":null,"evidenceType":null}],"source_locators":[{"sourceId":"url_a17e5b24c217aa80","title":"The Python Tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/","locator":"Language syntax, data structu