('subgroup', 'D078:economics-applied-analysis', 'D078', 'competency', 'D078-ECON-ECONOMIC-REASONING', 'Economic reasoning about choices, incentives, institutions, and trade-offs', 'The ability to use economic concepts to explain how choices and outcomes may respond to constraints, incentives, markets, and institutions.', 'This is economics-specific reasoning within D078�s broader study of people and institutions. It must not be treated as proof that a sociology programme teaches economic analysis. State the mechanism linking a policy or market change to an outcome, identify whose choices are affected, and name assumptions and competing explanations; output a clear analytical framework. This is economics-specific reasoning within D078’s broader study of people and institutions. It must not be treated as proof that a sociology programme teaches economic analysis. No curriculum transfer is asserted here. Route note: Show within the selected economics applied analysis area as a learner-selectable focus, not a compulsory mastery checkpoint. Task: State the mechanism linking a policy or market change to an outcome, identify whose choices are affected, and name assumptions and competing explanations; output a clear analytical framework. Guardrail: Models are conditional representations, not complete descriptions of an economy. A plausible mechanism does not establish the effect size or causality.', 'State the mechanism linking a policy or market change to an outcome, identify whose choices are affected, and name assumptions and competing explanations; output a clear analytical framework.', 'build_next', 'Show within the selected economics applied analysis area as a learner-selectable focus, not a compulsory mastery checkpoint. Task: State the mechanism linking a policy or market change to an outcome, identify whose choices are affected, and name assumptions and competing explanations; output a clear analytical framework. Guardrail: Models are conditional representations, not complete descriptions of an economy. A plausible mechanism does not establish the effect size or causality.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['ISCED_ECON', 'ONET_ECONOMIST', 'NISER_EIR_2026', 'OPENLEARN_ECON', 'CORE_DOING_ECONOMICS']::text[], '[{"id":"ISCED_ECON","title":"ISCED Fields of Education and Training 2013: Detailed Field Descriptions","url":"https://uis.unesco.org/sites/default/files/medias/fichiers/2025/04/international-standard-classification-of-education-fields-of-education-and-training-2013-detailed-field-descriptions-2015-en.pdf","region":"International","evidenceType":null},{"id":"ONET_ECONOMIST","title":"Economists, 19-3011.00","url":"https://www.onetonline.org/link/details/19-3011.00","region":"United States","evidenceType":null},{"id":"NISER_EIR_2026","title":"NISER Economic Intelligence Report 2026","url":"https://niser.gov.ng/v2/niser-economic-intelligence-report-2026/","region":"Nigeria; 2026 report","evidenceType":null},{"id":"OPENLEARN_ECON","title":"What is economics?","url":"https://www.open.edu/openlearn/society-politics-law/economics/what-economics","region":"Global learning resource","evidenceType":null},{"id":"CORE_DOING_ECONOMICS","title":"Doing Economics","url":"https://www.core-econ.org/project/doing-economics/","region":"Global open educational resource","evidenceType":null}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"ISCED_ECON","title":"ISCED Fields of Education and Training 2013: Detailed Field Descriptions","url":"https://uis.unesco.org/sites/default/files/medias/fichiers/2025/04/international-standard-classification-of-education-fields-of-education-and-training-2013-detailed-field-descriptions-2015-en.pdf","locator":"Field 0311 Economics; field description and exclusions","claimRole":null},{"sourceId":"ONET_ECONOMIST","title":"Economists, 19-3011.00","url":"https://www.onetonline.org/link/details/19-3011.00","locator":"Occupation summary and tasks: research economic issues, collect/analyse data, forecast trends and report findings","claimRole"