('subgroup', 'D078:economics-applied-analysis', 'D078', 'method', 'D078-ECON-CAUSAL-EVALUATION', 'Impact evaluation and causal-inference design', 'A set of designs for estimating what changed because of an intervention, by constructing a credible comparison for what would likely have happened without it.', 'Economics applies these methods to policy/programme questions, but methods are also used across social sciences; this area record owns the economic policy application. Define treatment, outcome, unit and time; choose an identification strategy (experimental or suitable quasi-experimental); examine assumptions, implementation and threats; report effect estimates and uncertainty. No programme link is asserted; CCMAS transfer and additional domain learning are separately tracked. Route note: Show within the selected economics applied analysis area as a learner-selectable focus, not a compulsory mastery checkpoint. Task: Define treatment, outcome, unit and time; choose an identification strategy (experimental or suitable quasi-experimental); examine assumptions, implementation and threats; report effect estimates and uncertainty. Guardrail: No method removes assumptions or implementation challenges. A statistically estimated effect may not generalize to another population or context.', 'Define treatment, outcome, unit and time; choose an identification strategy (experimental or suitable quasi-experimental); examine assumptions, implementation and threats; report effect estimates and uncertainty.', 'build_next', 'Show within the selected economics applied analysis area as a learner-selectable focus, not a compulsory mastery checkpoint. Task: Define treatment, outcome, unit and time; choose an identification strategy (experimental or suitable quasi-experimental); examine assumptions, implementation and threats; report effect estimates and uncertainty. Guardrail: No method removes assumptions or implementation challenges. A statistically estimated effect may not generalize to another population or context.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['WB_IMPACT_EVALUATION', 'AEA_CAUSAL_ECONOMETRICS', 'DIME_RESEARCH']::text[], '[{"id":"WB_IMPACT_EVALUATION","title":"Impact Evaluation in Practice, 2nd edition","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/programs/sief-trust-fund/publication/impact-evaluation-in-practice","region":"Global development-practice learning resource","evidenceType":null},{"id":"AEA_CAUSAL_ECONOMETRICS","title":"The State of Applied Econometrics: Causality and Policy Evaluation","url":"https://pubs.aeaweb.org/doi/10.1257/jep.31.2.3","region":"International economics research; examples/method debate not a universal protocol","evidenceType":null},{"id":"DIME_RESEARCH","title":"DIME research and impact evaluation working papers","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/about/unit/unit-dec/impactevaluation/dime-research","region":"Global development research","evidenceType":null}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"WB_IMPACT_EVALUATION","title":"Impact Evaluation in Practice, 2nd edition","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/programs/sief-trust-fund/publication/impact-evaluation-in-practice","locator":"Parts on evaluation questions, causal methods, implementation, sampling, data collection, ethics and dissemination; Chapters 3–11 include identification designs","claimRole":null},{"sourceId":"AEA_CAUSAL_ECONOMETRICS","title":"The State of Applied Econometrics: Causality and Policy Evaluation","url":"https://pubs.aeaweb.org/doi/10.1257/jep.31.2.3","locator":"Causal identification, supplementary robustness/sensitivity checks, external validity, and ML methods for causal effects","claimRole":null},{"sourceId":"DIME_RESEARCH","title":"DIME research and impact evaluation working papers","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/about/unit/unit-dec/impactevaluation/dime-research","locator":"Describes randomized and quasi-experimental impact evaluation methods in research portfolio","claimRole":null},{"sourceId":"WB_IMPACT_EVALUATION","title":"Impact Evaluation