('subgroup', 'D078:politics-governance-public-policy', 'D078', 'method', 'D078-A03-method-evaluate-ai-public-intervention', 'Impact evaluation of AI-enabled public interventions', 'A planned study of whether an AI system changes a public programme''s processes or outcomes, compared with what would likely have happened without it or with another approach.', 'Set out intervention logic, comparison/design, outcome and equity measures, implementation monitoring, human oversight and uncertainty. A specialization-specific policy/evaluation task; it does not make AI literacy a shared job requirement across all D078 areas. No programme curriculum transfer is asserted. Route note: Specialist evaluation route for a specific public intervention using AI; not a claim that AI use is inherently beneficial or lawful.', NULL, 'build_next', 'Specialist evaluation route for a specific public intervention using AI; not a claim that AI use is inherently beneficial or lawful.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['UK_MAGENTA_AI_EVALUATION', 'OECD_GOVERNING_AI_2025']::text[], '[{"id":"UK_MAGENTA_AI_EVALUATION","title":"Guidance on the Impact Evaluation of AI Interventions","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/guidance-on-the-impact-evaluation-of-ai-interventions-html","region":"United Kingdom central government; methodological reference, not Nigerian legal authority","evidenceType":null},{"id":"OECD_GOVERNING_AI_2025","title":"Governing with Artificial Intelligence: The State of Play and Way Forward in Core Government Functions","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/governing-with-artificial-intelligence_795de142-en.html","region":"International comparative policy evidence (OECD member and partner cases)","evidenceType":null}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"UK_MAGENTA_AI_EVALUATION","title":"Guidance on the Impact Evaluation of AI Interventions","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/guidance-on-the-impact-evaluation-of-ai-interventions-html","locator":"Updated 15 May 2026. Sections 1, 2.1–2.6 and conclusion: compare an AI intervention to a baseline/status quo; consider process, impact and value-for-money questions; use experimental, quasi-experimental or theory-based approaches proportionately; examine differences across groups, public attitudes, evolving systems and early baselines. Annex examples include document summarization and public-service chatbots.","claimRole":null},{"sourceId":"OECD_GOVERNING_AI_2025","title":"Governing with Artificial Intelligence: The State of Play and Way Forward in Core Government Functions","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/governing-with-artificial-intelligence_795de142-en.html","locator":"Full report, chapter on public policy evaluation, PDF printed p. 222 / report lines 8683–8714: AI support for ex-ante/ex-post evaluation and evidence synthesis; text mining has been used for literature searching, screening, extraction and analysis. The report also describes limits and risks of AI-supported analysis.","claimRole":null},{"sourceId":"UK_MAGENTA_AI_EVALUATION","title":"Guidance on the Impact Evaluation of AI Interventions","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/guidance-on-the-impact-evaluation-of-ai-interventions-html","locator":"Updated 15 May 2026. Sections 1, 2.1–2.6 and conclusion: compare an AI intervention to a baseline/status quo; consider process, impact and value-for-money questions; use experimental, quasi-experimental or theory-based approaches proportionately; examine differences across groups, public attitudes, evolving systems and early baselines. Annex examples include document summarization and public-service chatbots.","claimRole":null},{"sourceId":"UK_MAGENTA_AI_EVALUATION","title":"Guidance on the Impact Evaluation of AI Interventions","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/guidance-on-the-impact-evaluation-of-ai-interventions-html","locator":"Updated 15 May 2026. Sections 1, 2.1–2.6 and conclusion: compare an AI intervention to a baseline/status quo; consider process, impact and value-for-money questions; use experimental, quasi-experimental or theory-based approaches proportionately; examine differences across groups, public attitudes, evolving systems and early baselines. Annex examples include document summarization and public-service chatbots.","claimRole":"task_specific_learning_resource"}]'::jsonb, 'global', '["state","label","explanation","Specialist evaluation route for a specific public intervention using AI; not a claim that AI use is inherently beneficial or lawful."]'::jsonb, 'source_supported_synthesis', 'medium', 'source_backed', 'linked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/guidance-on-the-impact-evaluation-of-ai-interventions-html', 'Guidance on the Impact Evaluation of AI Interventions', '{"source_id":"UK_MAGENTA_AI_EVALUATION","publisher":"UK Government / HM Treasury and Evaluation Task Force","title":"Guidance on the Impact Evaluation of AI Interventions","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/guidance-on-the-impact-evaluation-of-ai-interventions-html","exact_locator":"Updated 15 May 2026. Sections 1, 2.1–2.6 and conclusion: compare an AI intervention to a baseline/status quo; consider process, impact and value-for-money questions; use experimental, quasi-experimental or theory-based approaches proportionately; examine differences across groups, public attitudes, evolving systems and early baselines. Annex examples include document summarization and public-service chatbots.","source_role":"task_validity_explanation_and_free_learning","jurisdiction":"United Kingdom central government; methodological reference, not Nigerian legal authority","supports":"Evaluation of AI-enabled public programmes/services is a distinct and growing policy-evaluation task; baseline, impact, heterogeneity and public perception should be considered.","limitations":"The document explicitly says it does not alone teach all evaluation skills or assess technical AI safety/benchmarks. Apply Nigerian law and institutional procedures separately.","access_date":"2026-10-10","locator":"Updated 15 May 2026. Sections 1, 2.1–2.6 and conclusion: compare an AI intervention to a baseline/status quo; consider process, impact and value-for-money questions; use experimental, quasi-experimental or theory-based approaches proportionately; examine differences across groups, public attitudes, evolving systems and early baselines. Annex examples include document summarization and public-service chatbots."}'::jsonb, '2026-10-12', 'medium', '{"url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/guidance-on-the-impact-evaluation-of-ai-interventions-html","locator":"Updated 15 May 2026. Sections 1, 2.1–2.6 and conclusion: compare an AI intervention to a baseline/status quo; consider process, impact and value-for-money questions; use experimental, quasi-experimental or theory-based approaches proportionately; examine differences across groups, public attitudes, evolving systems and early baselines. Annex examples include document summarization and public-service chatbots.","supports":"Evaluation of AI-enabled public programmes/services is a distinct and growing policy-evaluation task; baseline, impact, heterogeneity and public perception should be considered.","access":null}'::jsonb, NULL, 'subgroup', '2026-10-12', 'approved', '{"claims":{"item_identity":{"source_references":[{"id":"UK_MAGENTA_AI_EVALUATION","title":"Guidance on the Impact Evaluation of AI Interventions","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/guidance-on-the-impact-evaluation-of-ai-interventions-html","region":"United Kingdom central government; methodological reference, not Nigerian legal authority","evidenceType":null},{"id":"OECD_GOVERNING_AI_2025","title":"Governing with Artificial Intelligence: The State of Play and Way Forward in Core Government Functions","url":"http