('subgroup', 'D078:sociology-social-anthropology', 'D078', 'skill', 'D078-SOC-QUESTION-DESIGN', 'Question framing and research design', 'The ability to turn a broad concern about social life into a clear, answerable question and a design that can actually provide relevant evidence.', 'In this area it links sociological concepts such as institutions, inequality, norms, or social change to a studyable case. It is an area-specific use of the wider D078 research-design foundation, not a claim that one programme teaches the whole design skill. For example, replace �why are young people unemployed?� with a bounded question about how training access and household responsibilities relate to job search in a named population and period; produce a question, design rationale, and evidence plan. No programme-specific transfer is asserted by this record; connect it to a chosen programme only through its separately reviewed curriculum evidence. Route note: Show within the selected sociology social anthropology area as a learner-selectable focus, not a compulsory mastery checkpoint. Task: For example, replace �why are young people unemployed?� with a bounded question about how training access and household responsibilities relate to job search in a named population and period; produce a question, design rationale, and evidence plan. Guardrail: A well-written question cannot correct poor data or biased sampling. Do not assume a social association is causal.', 'For example, replace �why are young people unemployed?� with a bounded question about how training access and household responsibilities relate to job search in a named population and period; produce a question, design rationale, and evidence plan.', 'build_next', 'Show within the selected sociology social anthropology area as a learner-selectable focus, not a compulsory mastery checkpoint. Task: For example, replace �why are young people unemployed?� with a bounded question about how training access and household responsibilities relate to job search in a named population and period; produce a question, design rationale, and evidence plan. Guardrail: A well-written question cannot correct poor data or biased sampling. Do not assume a social association is causal.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['UNESCO_ISCED', 'UKDS_DATA_SKILLS', 'OPENSTAX_METHODS']::text[], '[{"id":"UNESCO_ISCED","title":"ISCED Fields of Education and Training 2013: Detailed Field Descriptions","url":"https://uis.unesco.org/sites/default/files/medias/fichiers/2025/04/international-standard-classification-of-education-fields-of-education-and-training-2013-detailed-field-descriptions-2015-en.pdf","region":"global/international","evidenceType":"international education classification"},{"id":"UKDS_DATA_SKILLS","title":"Data skills modules","url":"https://ukdataservice.ac.uk/learning-hub/data-skills-modules/","region":"Global learning resource; examples may be UK-specific","evidenceType":null},{"id":"OPENSTAX_METHODS","title":"Introduction to Sociology 3e, Research Methods section summary","url":"https://openstax.org/books/introduction-sociology-3e/pages/2-section-summary","region":"Global educational resource","evidenceType":null}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"UNESCO_ISCED","title":"ISCED Fields of Education and Training 2013: Detailed Field Descriptions","url":"https://uis.unesco.org/sites/default/files/medias/fichiers/2025/04/international-standard-classification-of-education-fields-of-education-and-training-2013-detailed-field-descriptions-2015-en.pdf","locator":"Fields 0311–0314 and adjacent inclusion/exclusion notes for economics, political science, psychology, sociology/cultural studies, demography and social geography","claimRole":"international education classification"},{"sourceId":"UKDS_DATA_SKILLS","title":"Data skills modules","url":"https://ukdataservice.ac.uk/learning-hub/data-skills-modules/","locator":"Free self-paced modules on surveys, longitudinal and aggregate data, ethical sourcing, anonymisation, consent