ory mastery checkpoint. Task: For example, replace �why are young people unemployed?� with a bounded question about how training access and household responsibilities relate to job search in a named population and period; produce a question, design rationale, and evidence plan. Guardrail: A well-written question cannot correct poor data or biased sampling. Do not assume a social association is causal.'),
('subgroup', 'D078:sociology-social-anthropology', 'D078', 'knowledge', 'D078-SOC-THEORY-CONTEXT', 'Sociological concepts and theory-informed interpretation', 'Knowledge of concepts and theoretical explanations used to describe relationships among people, groups, institutions, culture, power, and change.', 'This is distinctly sociological/anthropological when it explains social relations and institutions. It complements rather than replaces economic, psychological, or spatial explanations in interdisciplinary questions. Use concepts to make a reasoned interpretation of an observed pattern, compare explanations, and identify what evidence would support or challenge each account; output a concept-to-evidence explanation rather than name-dropping a theory. Specific theories should not be claimed as taught by a programme unless the reviewed curriculum names or supports them. Route note: Show within the selected sociology social anthropology area as a learner-selectable focus, not a compulsory mastery checkpoint. Task: Use concepts to make a reasoned interpretation of an observed pattern, compare explanations, and identify what evidence would support or challenge each account; output a concept-to-evidence explanation rather than name-dropping a theory. Guardrail: A framework can direct attention but may also obscure evidence that does not fit it. Theory does not by itself establish an empirical claim.', 'Use concepts to make a reasoned interpretation of an observed pattern, compare explanations, and identify what evidence would support or challenge each account; output a concept-to-evidence explanation rather than name-dropping a theory.', 'build_next', 'Show within the selected sociology social anthropology area as a learner-selectable focus, not a compulsory mastery checkpoint. Task: Use concepts to make a reasoned interpretation of an observed pattern, compare explanations, and identify what evidence would support or challenge each account; output a concept-to-evidence explanation rather than name-dropping a theory. Guardrail: A framework can direct attention but may also obscure evidence that does not fit it. Theory does not by itself establish an empirical claim.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['UNESCO_ISCED', 'OPENSTAX_METHODS']::text[], '[{"id":"UNESCO_ISCED","title":"ISCED Fields of Education and Training 2013: Detailed Field Descriptions","url":"https://uis.unesco.org/sites/default/files/medias/fichiers/2025/04/international-standard-classification-of-education-fields-of-education-and-training-2013-detailed-field-descriptions-2015-en.pdf","region":"global/international","evidenceType":"international education classification"},{"id":"OPENSTAX_METHODS","title":"Introduction to Sociology 3e, Research Methods section summary","url":"https://openstax.org/books/introduction-sociology-3e/pages/2-section-summary","region":"Global educational resource","evidenceType":null}]'::jsonb, '[{"sourceId":"UNESCO_ISCED","title":"ISCED Fields of Education and Training 2013: Detailed Field Descriptions","url":"https://uis.unesco.org/sites/default/files/medias/fichiers/2025/04/international-standard-classification-of-education-fields-of-education-and-training-2013-detailed-field-descriptions-2015-en.pdf","locator":"Fields 0311–0314 and adjacent inclusion/exclusion notes for economics, political science, psychology, sociology/cultural studies, demography and social geography","claimRole":"international education classification"},{"sourceId":"OPENSTAX_METHODS","title":"Introduction to Sociology 3e, Research Methods section summary","url":"https://openstax.org/books/introduction-sociology-3e/pages/2-section-summary","locator":"Research process, method selection, reliability/validity, ethics and social research methods","claimRole":null},{"sourceId":"OPENSTAX_METHODS","title":"Introduction to Sociology 3e, Research Methods section summary","url":"https://openstax.org/books/introduction-sociology-3e/pages/2-section-summary","locator":"Research pr