('subgroup', 'D078:sociology-social-anthropology', 'D078', 'method', 'D078-SOC-SOCIAL-NETWORK-ANALYSIS', 'Social network analysis (Gephi or R/Python alternatives)', 'A family of methods for describing relationships among people, groups, or organizations as nodes and ties, and studying properties such as connections, clusters, and network position.', 'Provides a relational lens on social organization; network measures are only meaningful when tie definitions and data collection match the social question. Create a documented node/edge table, define what counts as a tie, calculate question-appropriate network measures, and make a readable graph with interpretation cautions. An optional computational specialization; no curriculum link is inferred. Route note: Show only within the selected sociology social anthropology area and relevant use case, as an optional route. Task: Create a documented node/edge table, define what counts as a tie, calculate question-appropriate network measures, and make a readable graph with interpretation cautions. Guardrail: Centrality is not importance in every substantive sense. Missing ties, boundary choices, and privacy risk can distort conclusions.', 'Create a documented node/edge table, define what counts as a tie, calculate question-appropriate network measures, and make a readable graph with interpretation cautions.', 'useful_later', 'Show only within the selected sociology social anthropology area and relevant use case, as an optional route. Task: Create a documented node/edge table, define what counts as a tie, calculate question-appropriate network measures, and make a readable graph with interpretation cautions. Guardrail: Centrality is not importance in every substantive sense. Missing ties, boundary choices, and privacy risk can distort conclusions.', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['UKDS_COMPUTATIONAL', 'GEPHI_QUICKSTART', 'EDINBURGH_GEPHI_COURSE']::text[], '[{"id":"UKDS_COMPUTATIONAL","title":"Computational social science","url